% ============================================================================
% water-movement-route — which way through the Earth system a named water
% cycle process moves water (adj-facts-stdlib, EARTH-SCIENCE).
% ============================================================================
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? water_movement_route(runoff, $R)
%       % across_the_surface
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `water_movement_route(process, route)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the route WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls.
%
% THE BACKWARD QUERY IS THE ONE WORTH HAVING:
%
%     ? water_movement_route($P, into_the_ground)
%       % infiltration
%       % groundwater_recharge
%
% "Which processes move water into the ground?" is a real elementary
% question, and nothing in this stdlib could answer it before.
%
% *** NOT A DUPLICATE OF `water-cycle.adj`, AND THE DIFFERENCE MATTERS. ***
% `water_cycle_stage(process, step_number)` ORDERS five processes —
% evaporation 1, condensation 2, precipitation 3, runoff 4, groundwater 5 —
% and answers "what comes next?". This relation answers "WHICH WAY does
% water move?", covers EIGHT processes, and is not an ordering at all.
%
% The two tables overlap on THREE atoms — `evaporation`, `precipitation`
% and `runoff` — and disagree about nothing, because they are answering
% different questions about them. `runoff` is stage 4 there AND moves water
% across the surface here; both are true and neither implies the other.
% Five of this table's processes (evapotranspiration, snowmelt,
% streamflow, infiltration, groundwater_recharge) have no stage number,
% and `condensation`, which has one, has no route here (see the abstention
% below).
%
% Note `groundwater` (stage 5) and `groundwater_recharge` (a route value
% here) are DIFFERENT ATOMS naming different things — a reservoir versus a
% process — and are deliberately not unified. Neither source licenses
% treating them as the same thing.
%
% Provenance: THREE PARALLEL SENTENCES from the U.S. Geological Survey's
% Water Science School. The page states the whole relation in one
% structure, one sentence per route:
%
%     route                            processes
%     ------------------------------   -------------------------------
%     between_atmosphere_and_surface   evaporation, evapotranspiration,
%                                      precipitation
%     across_the_surface               snowmelt, runoff, streamflow
%     into_the_ground                  infiltration,
%                                      groundwater_recharge
%
% Each row's second column is the route its own sentence names, and every
% first-column atom is a term that sentence lists. Nothing is reworded and
% no process is assigned a route from general hydrology knowledge.
%
% The route atoms are compressed from the sentences' own phrasing —
% "between the atmosphere and the surface", "across the surface", "into
% the ground" — into single atoms. That is a naming choice, not a claim:
% the full phrasing is recoverable verbatim from the citation attached to
% every row, which is the point of carrying the citation.
%
% `trust authoritative` — the USGS is a first-party .gov publisher and the
% Water Science School is its educational reference. The page was
% CONTENT-verified by RAW TEXT EXTRACTION (HTTP 200, 84 substantive
% sentences, zero soft-404 markers), never from a fetch summary.
%
% THAT 84 IS THE ORIGINAL EXTRACTION'S FIGURE AND WAS NOT RE-MEASURED
% by the 2026-09-14 re-extraction below, which is worth saying plainly
% because it sits directly above a paragraph headed MEASURED, NOT
% ASSUMED. Re-counting on 2026-09-14 gives 79 sentences of 40 or more
% characters. That is NOT a correction: the rule behind the 84 was
% never recorded, so the two numbers are not comparable, and 79 is
% reported as what THIS count counted rather than as a replacement.
%
% PROVENANCE IS PER-ROW (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e), and with three sentences that
% is the whole point. Each row carries, in its own `row { source ... }`
% block, the sentence that LISTS IT. An answer about `runoff` now ships the
% SURFACE sentence as its primary source, and the atmosphere sentence does
% not reach it at all.
%
% Until this change, provenance was table-level: the `source` envelope was
% the ATMOSPHERE sentence and the other two were `cites` corroborations, so
% every row carried all three and `runoff` was warranted, as its PRIMARY
% source, by a sentence that does not mention runoff. In `--explain`, which
% renders the primary source and drops corroborations, the runoff row
% displayed under the atmosphere sentence. Nothing cited was false and the
% grounding sentence was always among the three, but matching sentence to
% row was left to the reader. That is issue #14986, and for THIS table it is
% now fixed; the claim is about this file and no other.
%
% THREE ROWS SHARE A SENTENCE, and that is honest rather than a compromise:
% the atmosphere sentence lists evaporation, evapotranspiration AND
% precipitation by name, so it grounds each of those three rows equally.
% Sharing a span is only a defect when the span does not name the row.
%
% THE ENVELOPE IS A FRAMING SENTENCE — "The water cycle describes where
% water is on Earth and how it moves." — chosen because it names NONE of
% the eight row keys and so cannot mis-warrant any of them. `source` is a
% REQUIRED envelope field (an envelope carrying only `locator` and `trust`
% is rejected with `TableMissingProvenance`), so the slot must be filled by
% something; filling it with one of the three route sentences is exactly
% what produced the defect above.
%
% MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. Re-extracted 2026-09-14 (HTTP 200; 95,733
% bytes on disk, 95,701 Unicode characters — the two are stated
% separately because one line below this one says `byte-faithful`, and
% an earlier wording labelled the CHARACTER count as bytes):
% all three route sentences occur verbatim, all three inside `<p>` blocks,
% and a fabricated control sentence is reported absent by the same
% comparison. The three are CONSECUTIVE sentences of ONE `<p>` — which is
% what "three parallel sentences" above had always asserted and never
% shown.
%
% ONE EXTRACTION HAZARD WORTH RECORDING, because it nearly put a wrong
% string in the `source` envelope. The three sentences wrap each process
% name in a link — `<a>evaporation</a>, <a>evapotranspiration</a>` — so
% naive tag-stripping yields "evaporation , evapotranspiration" with
% spaces the page does not contain. The sentences below were checked
% against the RAW HTML and carry the real punctuation. A citation is only
% worth having if it is byte-faithful.
%
% Honest abstention:
%
%   - CONDENSATION is NOT a row, which is the abstention most worth
%     understanding. It is unmistakably a water cycle process — it is
%     stage 2 in `water-cycle.adj` — and the page discusses it. But NONE
%     of the three route sentences lists it, and this relation's values
%     come from those sentences alone. Assigning it a route would mean
%     reasoning about where condensation happens rather than reporting
%     what the source says, and the fact that a process is famous is not
%     evidence about this relation.
%   - SUBLIMATION is not a row, for the same reason: the page names it
%     once and never places it in a route sentence.
%   - TRANSPIRATION alone is not a row. The sentence lists
%     `evapotranspiration`, and that compound is the atom. Splitting it
%     into transpiration and evaporation would assert a decomposition this
%     sentence does not make.
%   - The three routes are not claimed to be exhaustive or exclusive. The
%     source states three; a process absent here means these sentences did
%     not list it, NOT that water never moves that way.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table water_movement_route {
    columns process, route

    % Each row carries the sentence that LISTS IT BY NAME. The three
    % sentences are consecutive sentences of one paragraph, and each
    % names every process on its own route, so three rows sharing the
    % atmosphere sentence are each warranted by it -- not approximated
    % by it. NO ROW RESTATES `locator`: every sentence is on the one
    % page the envelope names, and the rule
    % `geography/reference-lines.adj` states is to restate a row locator
    % only when its page DIFFERS from the envelope's.
    row (evaporation, between_atmosphere_and_surface) {
        source "Water moves between the atmosphere and the surface through evaporation, evapotranspiration, and precipitation."
    }
    row (evapotranspiration, between_atmosphere_and_surface) {
        source "Water moves between the atmosphere and the surface through evaporation, evapotranspiration, and precipitation."
    }
    row (precipitation, between_atmosphere_and_surface) {
        source "Water moves between the atmosphere and the surface through evaporation, evapotranspiration, and precipitation."
    }

    row (snowmelt, across_the_surface) {
        source "Water moves across the surface through snowmelt, runoff, and streamflow."
    }
    row (runoff, across_the_surface) {
        source "Water moves across the surface through snowmelt, runoff, and streamflow."
    }
    row (streamflow, across_the_surface) {
        source "Water moves across the surface through snowmelt, runoff, and streamflow."
    }

    row (infiltration, into_the_ground) {
        source "Water moves into the ground through infiltration and groundwater recharge."
    }
    row (groundwater_recharge, into_the_ground) {
        source "Water moves into the ground through infiltration and groundwater recharge."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE NAMES NO PROCESS, which is the property that makes it
    % safe in the framing slot: it cannot mis-warrant a row, because it
    % mentions none of them. Checked against all eight row keys in the
    % page's own wording. This sentence occurs twice in the page TEXT,
    % both times in narrative `<p>` blocks — and four times in the raw
    % HTML, because the other two are the `<meta name="description">`
    % and `<meta property="og:description">` tags.
    source "The water cycle describes where water is on Earth and how it moves."
    locator "https://www.usgs.gov/water-science-school/water-cycle"
    trust authoritative
}
